WEBVTT

0
00:00:05.079 --> 00:00:08.289
Modern manufacturing facilities run on two distinct networks.

1
00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:12.579
The IT network supports business operations such as email,

2
00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:13.800
enterprise systems,

3
00:00:13.979 --> 00:00:15.260
and corporate applications.

4
00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:19.049
The OT network runs the physical processes on the plant floor,

5
00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:19.719
sensors,

6
00:00:19.969 --> 00:00:20.280
controllers,

7
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:20.969
and machines.

8
00:00:22.659 --> 00:00:23.979
Within the OT network,

9
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:25.860
devices are organized into levels,

10
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:27.680
each serving a specific function

11
00:00:28.059 --> 00:00:29.940
with communication controlled between them.

12
00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:32.150
In a well segmented network,

13
00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:34.909
what is connected at each level is known and documented.

14
00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.659
As IT and OT converge,

15
00:00:37.779 --> 00:00:40.799
maintaining visibility into what is connected becomes complex.

16
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.310
At this industrial facility,

17
00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:45.630
operations recently expanded,

18
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.909
bringing on new staff and new equipment.

19
00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:51.990
Sam is an IT network engineer whose responsibilities now include

20
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.650
the OT network.

21
00:00:54.549 --> 00:00:56.979
Sam pulls up the documented network topology.

22
00:00:57.389 --> 00:00:58.430
According to the diagram,

23
00:00:58.509 --> 00:01:01.290
there are 2,585 devices

24
00:01:01.509 --> 00:01:02.799
on the OT network,

25
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.209
PLCs,

26
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.540
HMIs,

27
00:01:05.709 --> 00:01:06.379
sensors,

28
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:07.260
historians,

29
00:01:07.349 --> 00:01:07.949
and more

30
00:01:08.269 --> 00:01:10.750
distributed across the levels of the control hierarchy.

31
00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.580
As part of the expansion,

32
00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.260
the facility has deployed Cisco Cyber Vision.

33
00:01:15.919 --> 00:01:18.230
It automatically inventories what is connected to the

34
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:21.160
OT network and monitors how those devices communicate.

35
00:01:22.029 --> 00:01:23.910
Sam connects it and lets it run.

36
00:01:25.419 --> 00:01:28.629
Sam expected 2,585 devices.

37
00:01:29.019 --> 00:01:31.860
Cybervision finds 2,603.

38
00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:37.199
The extra 18 include an HVAC control unit with an active outbound connection

39
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:38.550
to an outside network,

40
00:01:38.839 --> 00:01:40.839
a consumer device plugged into a break room

41
00:01:40.839 --> 00:01:43.199
jack that traces back through the OT segment,

42
00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.800
and several mobile devices generating traffic from the plant floor.

43
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.069
None of these belong at the levels where they were found.

44
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.449
In a properly segmented architecture,

45
00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.910
devices in the OT zone should be there,

46
00:01:55.569 --> 00:01:57.569
because they are part of the controlled process,

47
00:01:57.769 --> 00:02:00.690
not because someone plugged something in and the network accepted it.

48
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.750
Standard IT monitoring tools would not have caught this.

49
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.729
IT discovery tools are built to query and interpret IT protocol responses,

50
00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.570
but OT protocols run at the application layer

51
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:15.809
on top of standard Ethernet and IP infrastructure.

52
00:02:16.639 --> 00:02:18.389
Cyber Vision works differently.

53
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.720
It performs deep packet inspection on traffic already flowing across the network,

54
00:02:23.119 --> 00:02:26.850
building a complete picture without requiring devices to respond to queries.

55
00:02:27.779 --> 00:02:29.979
That picture surfaces the problems.

56
00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:35.440
The HVAC unit's outbound connection crosses the OT zone boundary.

57
00:02:35.839 --> 00:02:39.679
The consumer device is inside a segment it has no business being in.

58
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.110
The mobile device traffic has no documented source.

59
00:02:43.479 --> 00:02:46.759
These are exactly the kinds of zone boundary violations that the

60
00:02:46.759 --> 00:02:52.199
IEC 62443 zones and conduits model is designed to prevent,

61
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.899
and visibility is what makes them actionable.

62
00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:57.850
You can't secure what you can't see.

63
00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:01.229
Knowing what is on the network is the starting point.

64
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:03.770
The next section goes deeper into the tools that

65
00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:07.240
security teams use to gain visibility into OT networks.

66
00:03:07.589 --> 00:03:09.649
Investigate what devices have been doing,

67
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.000
correlate activity across data sources,

68
00:03:12.009 --> 00:03:13.809
and turn findings into response.
